// boxcxx — <valarray>  ([numarray])
//
// The array of numbers you write arithmetic on. Everything is in
// <__bits/valarray_core>, and that split is not cosmetic: the leaf is
// self-contained — the brigade declarations, <cmath>, <cstdlib>, <cstddef>,
// <initializer_list>, <new>, <type_traits>, and nothing of boxcxx's own
// containers — so the host stand can build it beside libstdc++ and libc++
// through symlinks and answer a question in a second instead of in a boot
// (tools/cxx_valarray_oracle.sh).
//
// Two things about this implementation are worth knowing before you use it,
// and both are in the leaf's own header comment at length:
//
//   * An expression is a WORK ORDER. `a + b * c` computes nothing; the pass
//     happens where the order is written into an array, so three operators
//     cost one pass over memory and no temporaries. [valarray.overview]/4
//     allows this, and both reference libraries do the same. `auto x = a + b`
//     therefore gives you the order, not an array — name the type, or the
//     order outlives what it reads.
//   * The pass is run by the BRIGADE — the strands std::execution::par uses —
//     when the array is long enough to be worth a wake. Neither reference
//     does that; on this system a long valarray expression is the cheapest
//     way there is to put every core to work. See §2 <valarray> in
//     CONFORMANCE.md for what that changes about apply() and sum().
//
// [version.syn] gives <valarray> no feature-test macro of its own, so this
// header declares none.
#ifndef BOXCXX_VALARRAY
#define BOXCXX_VALARRAY

#include <__bits/valarray_core>

#endif // BOXCXX_VALARRAY
